  "summary": "Michigan City, Indiana officials and Flock Safety representatives held a public meeting to address resident privacy concerns about the city's expanding ALPR camera network and a proposed Drone as First Responder program. The city has used Flock cameras since 2023, growing to 60 cameras at $280,000 annually, and the proposed drone program would add roughly $140,000 per year. Officials described data retention (30 days), no federal sharing, and use limited to criminal investigations.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Flock Safety representatives said the cameras collect publicly visible information, comparing the technology to \"an officer standing by a roadway and taking pictures of license plates 24/7, 365.\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "The company said data collected by the cameras is not shared with the federal government, is only accessed as part of criminal investigations and is automatically deleted after 30 days under the terms of its contract with the city.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "The city's current contract with Flock Safety costs approximately $280,000 annually from the general fund. Adding the Drone as First Responder program would increase that cost by about $140,000 per year.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    }
  ],
